clean-arch/PRD-HISTORY.md:152:A [source-provenance census](STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md) found 163 records across three explicitly recognizable page components where generated-summary content was retained but its label was removed. This is a bounded source-structure count, not a count of model errors. On two NVIDIA pages, the cleaner's template removal and long-line restoration reproduce the mismatch; a general provenance-preserving handoff is under review. An offline probe reproduced the current cleaned text for 163 affected pages and 200 seed-selected additional pages. It retains all candidate original positions, marks transformed bylines, and shows one missing-HTML fallback; positions do not by themselves establish source role. [Lineage replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/review.html). Source content remains available and no production data or prompt has been changed.
clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md:84:[Round 24](../poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/round-24-response.md) traced the current source pipeline and proposed an offline alignment probe. In [round 25](../poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/round-25-response.md), Claude withdrew a fixed two-line proximity rule: source labels may precede or follow content, and adjacency does not establish scope. Independent review identified duplicate lines, joined bylines, missing-HTML and source-offset pitfalls; the [probe design](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/design.md) now preserves all possible matches and uncertainty. The original and cleaned databases, HTML index and current extractor version have been identified; identical current recomputation does not prove independently signed historical HTML bytes. No source labels are automatically interpreted as a model rule and no source database is changed.
clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md:28:An offline identity probe replaced the ASCII-only lookup key with Unicode `isalnum()` characters while keeping every other cleaner operation the same. The existing cleaner reproduced all 6,463 saved fed-article texts with current saved HTML. The candidate changed eight pages, removing ten lines and adding none: seven English titles with a Cyrillic publisher suffix and three Thai page-disclosure or interface lines. The [full changed lines](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/unicode-identity-corpus.jsonl) are preserved; this is not a judgment that all removals are safe or unimportant. The candidate is **not adopted**: `isalnum()` drops combining marks, so distinct Thai strings such as `กา` and `ก่า` have the same candidate key, as do some distinct Devanagari strings. It therefore does not supply reliable multilingual identity. A repair must preserve meaningful marks and examine both complete and partial-key collisions, existing quotations and all changed outputs before any integration. [Full-corpus probe](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/unicode-identity-corpus-summary.json).
clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md:30:A second offline key retained Unicode letters, numbers and marks after NFKC normalization. On the same 6,463 articles it changed 11 pages: two lines added and 11 removed, with the existing cleaner again reproducing all saved texts. The two additions are Thai gambling-page titles on pages whose stored source titles describe oil markets; this illustrates that improved text matching cannot establish that the captured page is the intended article. Another removal was a phrase inside a generated-analysis component. The [complete changes](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/unicode-marks-corpus.jsonl) require source-role and downstream review. This candidate has not been adopted; preserving marks does not by itself prove a complete Unicode identity contract.
clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md:32:A reproducible [sample of surrounding page text](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/sample-contexts.txt) shows why restoring every matched line would be unsafe. In one page, extractor-matched lines are links to other articles; other examples include code, lists and a section heading. Among long unmatched lines are advertisements, page titles and later-story teasers. These are selected structural examples; they do not quantify relevance or contamination across the full corpus. Nineteen long unmatched lines ending in `®` occur on 19 pages from one publisher, including article 16; this does not justify a publisher- or symbol-specific production exception.
clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md:42:Claude withdrew the symbol proposal in its [second response](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round2-response.md) and suggested examining the existing HTML paragraph/aside diagnostic. The [all-fed-article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/html-v2-corpus-summary.json) reproduces all 49 previously saved diagnostic diffs and applies its same line-matching and page-tag logic across 6,463 originally fed articles. Thirty-two lack HTML; the candidate changes 1,054 pages, adding 2,816 line values and removing 296. It is a list of proposed changes, not constructed model inputs or a measure of accuracy. A sampled addition on `gnews:085fc8af40b0bd255bb43131` is a film story on a page whose source title concerns a BRICS summit, and other additions include promotional links. Some removals are bylines for related stories, but all removed lines require review in their source context before any claim of safety. The candidate also relies on the existing lossy ASCII matcher. The complete [per-page changes](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/html-v2-corpus.jsonl) are saved. The 49-article positive cases alone do not validate this approach.
