 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Step 1 source provenance: missing generated-summary labels
     2	
     3	## Confirmed source-preparation mechanism
     4	
     5	Two NVIDIA technical pages in the preregistered comparison contain an explicitly labeled AI-generated summary in the captured page and original HTML. Their cleaned `text` retains summary sentences but not the label. The Step 1 request sends the cleaned text, so Luna cannot tell from that text that those sentences came from the generated-summary component. The body may independently support some of the same statements; the missing label does not make every extracted statement false.
     6	
     7	The saved cleaner first removes common site-template lines and short fragments in [`clean_pages.py`](../poc/corpus/scripts/clean_pages.py). It then retains sufficiently long sentence-like rule-cleaned lines even when the article extractor omits them. It does not re-add lines removed by the first pass. On the two inspected NVIDIA pages, the repeated short label is removed while four longer summary lines survive that fallback; the original functions reproduce the stored cleaned text. This is a source-handoff provenance defect. Turning off the long-line retention fallback is not a solution: prior review found it also recovers genuine article content such as financial guidance.
     8	
     9	## Bounded corpus census
    10	
    11	The [reproducible census](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-provenance-2026-11-07/census.py) reads all 34,084 saved corpus rows and matches three explicitly bounded page structures. It counts retained exact flattened-page lines and checks known positive controls. It does not decide whether a claim is useful or correct.
    12	
    13	| Explicit page component | Pages with missing label and retained component content | Component lines in captured pages | Exact lines retained in cleaned text |
    14	|---|---:|---:|---:|
    15	| NVIDIA `AI-Generated Summary`, bounded by `Next Steps` and a later NVIDIA Nemotron disclosure | 149 | 759 | 748 |
    16	| TradingKey `Overview Key Points` followed by `AI-generated summary` | 12 | 12 | 12 |
    17	| Cisco `Summary is AI-generated, editor-reviewed.` and the following three takeaway lines | 2 | 6 | 2 |
    18	| **Total for these three structures** | **163** | **777** | **762** |
    19	
    20	These are source records with an identifiable label/content mismatch, not 163 model errors or a system-wide rate. The census does not characterize other label formats, incompletely captured pages, content that was reflowed into different lines, or every source publisher. Another 54 TradingView pages have an AI-related disclosure, but their content boundaries have not been established and they are excluded from this count. The Cisco boundary is intentionally limited to the three observed takeaway lines. Within these bounded components, none of the 762 retained exact lines occurs more than once as an identical whole line in its captured page. This does not rule out paraphrases, partial overlap or ambiguity elsewhere. The archived HTML was independently checked for one page from each of the three structures; that does not establish HTML availability for all 163 pages. The original captured HTML can contain richer structure than flattened `text_page`; availability and quality vary by source.
    21	
    22	## Handling and open question
    23	
    24	The preregistered replay flags the two known affected pages for the human reader and preserves the original answers; no source content is removed. Across the 163 bounded affected records, cleaned text totals 2,281,395 characters and captured page text totals 2,997,581 characters; median lengths are 14,372 and 18,543 characters. These are character counts for this selected class, not token, cost or latency estimates. Sending the full page would retain labels in these captures but can also expose navigation, unrelated stories, stale widgets and flattened ambiguity; it has not been validated as a general solution. A general solution needs to preserve a source's own provenance labels together with the content they qualify, including labels that precede or follow their content. It must also retain useful article text, source order and separately identifiable quotations; unknown or ambiguous boundaries must remain visible rather than being asserted as verified. The earlier model-proposed source-role selector reduced particular contamination cases but also misclassified some introductions and metadata, so its output is not a general solution.
    25	
    26	Codex and Claude Code are reviewing the existing cleaner, source captures and downstream request together before any implementation. No database, frozen production configuration or model prompt has been modified to address this issue. See the [source-handoff council](STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) and the [additional-source replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-date-unseen-2026-11-07/review.html).
    27	
    28	## Offline source-lineage replay
    29	
    30	The [lineage replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/review.html) covers the 163 bounded affected records and 200 non-overlapping feed articles selected by a recorded hash seed. An independent reader reran the actual current cleaner and verified the raw and cleaned records: all 363 outputs matched the saved cleaned text. Current HTML was available for 362 pages; the remaining page reproduced through the cleaner's existing no-HTML fallback. Current database and HTML-index hashes remained unchanged during the run. This establishes reproduction with the current saved inputs and extractor, not independent proof that every historical HTML byte is unchanged.
    31	
    32	Across 15,280 cleaned lines, 15,026 have one exact whole-line candidate in the captured page, 237 have multiple exact candidates, and 17 have a transformed joined-byline candidate. A single exact text candidate is a location observation; multiple candidates do not reveal which occurrence was retained, and a joined line is not a verbatim original line. The [raw lineage records](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/lineage.jsonl) preserve the page and cleaned text, candidate positions, raw character offsets and extractor observations. A known citation is contiguous in cleaned text but not in the captured page; both sets of exact offsets are retained without changing the evidence.
    33	
    34	Among the 762 retained lines in the bounded components, 746 NVIDIA lines and two Cisco lines did not meet the cleaner's normalized extractor-text match and had its long-sentence shape; with a nonempty extractor result, that is consistent with the existing long-line retention fallback. Twelve TradingKey lines met the cleaner's extractor-text match. An extractor match checks normalized text, not authorship or semantic provenance. The nearest identified label was one nonempty captured-page line away on 158 affected pages, two on four and three on one. Distance is not evidence of a label's scope, and these three explicit structures do not cover arbitrary publishers. [Reproducible analysis](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/analysis.json).
    35	
    36	The 200 additional selected articles contain 1,933,930 captured-page characters and 923,342 cleaned-text characters. Across these pages, the median captured page has 168.5 nonempty line positions without an exact or joined-byline candidate in the cleaned output. This is an [observation of mapping and size](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/context-sizes.json), not a count of irrelevant or definitely deleted lines; content may be transformed or reflowed. It does not measure tokens or model performance.
    37	
    38	This probe does not select or suppress content for Step 1, classify passages, alter the prompt or add a production handoff. The next design decision is how much original page context the extractor should receive without misrepresenting its scope or overwhelming it with unrelated material; a controlled comparison and source-backed review are required.
     1	# Step 1 source context: offline observations
     2	
     3	## Result
     4	
     5	A simple “restore source lines next to a long line retained by the cleaner’s fallback” approach is **not a complete fix** for missing source labels. On the 163 previously identified pages, considering only the immediate original-page neighbors would expose the known label on 144 pages; considering the full adjacent unmatched runs would expose it on 149. Neither covers the 12 TradingKey pages, where the retained summary matched the extractor rather than the long-line retention fallback. Other cases are blocked by an intervening retained line. These are counts for three known page structures, not a corpus-wide estimate.
     6	
     7	This was an offline observation: no source database, model input, prompt or evidence was changed. The [source-context viewer](../poc/data/clean-step1-context-restoration-2026-11-07/review.html) shows all 363 pages, prospective original-page positions and existing cleaned text. The [lineage replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-lineage-2026-11-07/review.html) shows all candidate source positions. Page adjacency does not establish whether a label applies to a particular paragraph, and the observations do not automatically classify content.
     8	
     9	## Coverage and added context
    10	
    11	| Frozen cohort | Pages | Immediate-neighbor positions | Full-adjacent-run positions |
    12	|---|---:|---:|---:|
    13	| 163 known label-loss pages | 163 | 1,246 | 8,427 |
    14	| Additional seed-selected feed articles | 200 | 386 | 6,130 |
    15	
    16	The full-run figures include the immediate-neighbor positions; they are not additional to them. One of the 200 additional pages had no saved HTML and could not be assessed for this extractor-dependent anchor. Multiple possible source positions are treated as conservative stopping points, not confirmed retained occurrences. The [raw observations](../poc/data/clean-step1-context-restoration-2026-11-07/observations.json) retain page IDs, source positions, ambiguous stops, extraction status and the exact selection.
    17	
    18	Thirty pages were selected by a fixed hash before their proposed lines were reviewed. Three independent readers inspected every proposed line in those pages against the original page and its context. Nineteen pages had proposals; eleven had none. Across the thirty, full runs contained 1,102 positions, including 109 also selected by the immediate-neighbor approach. These are observations of a fixed review sample, not a population rate.
    19	
    20	The recovered text includes real provenance and useful context, including generated-summary labels, an editor disclosure, authors and dates, source links, technical resources, and sponsorship labels. It also includes menus, social controls, advertising, unrelated recommendations and, on at least one page, stories dated after the original article. Some headings have ambiguous scope. For example, NVIDIA's “AI-Generated Summary” label is associated with a summary component; adjacent “Next Steps,” author information and the later article body must not automatically inherit that label. A sponsored label is useful provenance but does not establish that a particular claim is false.
    21	
    22	An Ising quote previously appeared contiguous in the cleaned text while two checkpoint-name lines intervened in the captured page. Neither observed approach proposed those two lines. An offline replay constructed hypothetical texts for three overlapping articles whose cleaned lines have unique, ordered page matches. It rechecked 18 saved answers containing 153 passages with the current clean Step 1 validator. For each approach, all 151 previously exact passages remained exact and both previously unverified passages remained unverified; no returned quote text changed. The Ising passage stayed falsely exact because neither approach restored the intervening checkpoint lines. As a separate control, checking the same saved passages against the captured full page left 150 exact and two unverified unchanged; the Ising passage became unverified. This detects the missing intervening text in that example, but neither confirms quotation meaning nor tests what the model would extract from the full page. This is a selected three-article compatibility observation, not a safety guarantee: exact matching proves presence in the supplied text, not faithful continuity in the original page. The reconstructed texts are temporary diagnostic inputs with old metadata, not production records. The [replay and passage results](../poc/data/clean-step1-context-restoration-2026-11-07/quote-replay.json) are saved alongside the [script](../poc/data/clean-step1-context-restoration-2026-11-07/replay_quotes.py).
    23	
    24	No model quality claim follows from these observations. The next design must preserve useful context and source identity without blindly adding all page material, guessing a label's scope, deleting evidence or adding a relevance gate. Existing ambiguous or missing original positions must remain visible.
    25	
    26	## Historical saved quotation census
    27	
    28	An independent read-only count of the two original forward-play traces found 49 completed articles containing 250 passage instances: 248 stored verified passages also occurred contiguously in the saved captured page, and two passages were already unverified in the cleaned input. One failed trace is excluded. All 49 completed items had captured page text. An independent reader checked the source items, requests, both non-locating examples and file hashes. This is a location check of saved instances, not an estimate for all articles or proof of source role, completeness or meaning. The separate eight-article comparison contained 296 passage instances, including one clean-only continuous match and two unverified passages. [Census and source hashes](../poc/data/clean-step1-original-quote-census-2026-11-07/results.json); [reproduction](../poc/data/clean-step1-original-quote-census-2026-11-07/census.py).
     1	# Step 1 source loss: cleaner investigation
     2	
     3	## Two traced cases
     4	
     5	The saved source-to-input path loses real article text in two different ways. This is a review of the historical corpus cleaner and the isolated clean Step 1 input, not a change to the source database or the production configuration.
     6	
     7	- **Article 2 — a reported outlook assumption.** The captured page says that NVIDIA did not include China revenue from the relevant market in its outlook, according to its CFO. The text was absent from the clean input. The current HTML extractor returns that line, but the first cleaning pass removes it before the extractor match is considered. After earlier filters, this line is followed by two related-story headlines; all three are at most 110 characters, lack an accepted ending character, contain no digit, and form a headline run. The first-pass rule removes the run. This is not a Luna omission: Luna did not receive the line. The raw page also contains an AI-generated summary label and related stories; a correct financial sentence is not proof that the whole surrounding page is article body.
     8	- **Article 16 — financing.** The captured page reports the first close of a $1 billion Series F at an $11 billion valuation. This 282-character line survives the first pass, but is not matched by the current HTML extractor. The fallback keeps long unmatched lines only if they end with accepted punctuation; this one ends in `®` and is removed. The saved cleaner output is reproduced. This diagnoses a source loss, not the model's later decision: in a separate prepared-text comparison the financing was restored but omitted from all three answers. Another article had previously supplied related financing, so novelty in the stateful run is not established.
     9	
    10	The inputs are the original saved page database and cleaned database: `gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c` and `gnews:b2ff1fae25f82fd3111134de`. The cleaner is [`clean_pages.py`](../poc/corpus/scripts/clean_pages.py), including `clean_lines`, `without_headline_runs`, and `clean_article`. The saved HTML is the current archive, not an independently signed copy of every original historical HTML byte.
    11	
    12	## Bounded corpus observations
    13	
    14	The [$0 census](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/results.json) read all 34,084 original source rows and considered the 6,463 rows that were originally fed articles. It reconstructed publisher templates from the original database. Two known case controls were checked. Counts below describe rule conditions and exact cleaned-line presence, **not counts of material investment facts or model errors**:
    15	
    16	| Observed cleaner condition | Pages | Line values |
    17	|---|---:|---:|
    18	| Removed by the headline-run rule | 2,129 | 12,101 |
    19	| Survived the first pass, at least 120 characters, no accepted ending | 2,335 | 5,770 |
    20	| Of the latter, absent as an exact line from the saved clean text | — | 3,703 |
    21	
    22	The [extractor comparison](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/extractor-summary.json) then examined the 3,567 pages with at least one of these candidates using the current stored HTML and trafilatura 2.3.1. It recorded nonempty extractor output for 3,538, no HTML for nine and empty output for 20. Among classified headline-removed lines, 3,287 on 965 pages match the cleaner's normalized extractor lookup and are absent from the saved output. Among classified long lines without an accepted ending, 3,703 lack an extractor match and are absent from the saved output. Exact rule inputs and outputs, input hashes, a current HTML index hash, decoded HTML hashes and full candidate lines are preserved in the [work area](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/). A normalized extractor match uses the cleaner's own prefix rule and does not establish full-line identity, relevance, source role or correct article boundaries.
    23	
    24	An independent audit found an additional matching limitation. The cleaner's `_letters` function removes non-ASCII letters; 11 of the 3,287 headline matches and 11 other long-line matches in this selected census have an empty normalized key. An empty key can match an empty extractor line even when the source sentence is absent from the extractor output. Two Chinese lines in `gnews:fdc4e0fba86169e7cc72754a` demonstrate that failure. The 22 are empty-key matches, not necessarily 22 false matches; this count covers only the candidates above, not all cleaned lines. A general repair remains unvalidated.
    25	
    26	A separate [all-fed-article empty-key census](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/empty-key-summary.json) examined rule-surviving lines rather than headline-removed values: seven of 6,463 source articles contain 24 such line values. On three pages, the extractor's short-line set contains an empty normalized key; 15 values on those pages are in the saved cleaned text. Three of those 15 values, all from one page, are not verbatim substrings of the current extractor output and do not independently meet the long-line fallback. They are a Thai disclaimer and interface instructions, not established lost investment evidence. Exact nonoccurrence does not rule out reflow or a semantic equivalent; current extractor output is not independently guaranteed to reproduce historical HTML. This census does not measure partially normalized multilingual lines. A first attempt used a control removed by the preceding headline rule and failed its own precondition; the control was corrected to a rule-surviving Thai line, and the completed census was rerun.
    27	
    28	An offline identity probe replaced the ASCII-only lookup key with Unicode `isalnum()` characters while keeping every other cleaner operation the same. The existing cleaner reproduced all 6,463 saved fed-article texts with current saved HTML. The candidate changed eight pages, removing ten lines and adding none: seven English titles with a Cyrillic publisher suffix and three Thai page-disclosure or interface lines. The [full changed lines](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/unicode-identity-corpus.jsonl) are preserved; this is not a judgment that all removals are safe or unimportant. The candidate is **not adopted**: `isalnum()` drops combining marks, so distinct Thai strings such as `กา` and `ก่า` have the same candidate key, as do some distinct Devanagari strings. It therefore does not supply reliable multilingual identity. A repair must preserve meaningful marks and examine both complete and partial-key collisions, existing quotations and all changed outputs before any integration. [Full-corpus probe](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/unicode-identity-corpus-summary.json).
    29	
    30	A second offline key retained Unicode letters, numbers and marks after NFKC normalization. On the same 6,463 articles it changed 11 pages: two lines added and 11 removed, with the existing cleaner again reproducing all saved texts. The two additions are Thai gambling-page titles on pages whose stored source titles describe oil markets; this illustrates that improved text matching cannot establish that the captured page is the intended article. Another removal was a phrase inside a generated-analysis component. The [complete changes](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/unicode-marks-corpus.jsonl) require source-role and downstream review. This candidate has not been adopted; preserving marks does not by itself prove a complete Unicode identity contract.
    31	
    32	A reproducible [sample of surrounding page text](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/sample-contexts.txt) shows why restoring every matched line would be unsafe. In one page, extractor-matched lines are links to other articles; other examples include code, lists and a section heading. Among long unmatched lines are advertisements, page titles and later-story teasers. These are selected structural examples; they do not quantify relevance or contamination across the full corpus. Nineteen long unmatched lines ending in `®` occur on 19 pages from one publisher, including article 16; this does not justify a publisher- or symbol-specific production exception.
    33	
    34	Both missing sentences appear inside an HTML `article` element in their currently saved pages, but article 2's element also contains related-story links and article 16's page has twelve `article` elements. On the previously frozen 363-page lineage cohort, 362 saved pages were parseable: 196 had no `article` element, 105 had one and 61 had multiple. This cohort deliberately includes 163 pages from known affected structures and is not a representative population sample. A normalized title appeared in exactly one such element on 118 pages; title containment alone does not prove the right source or body. The [DOM observation](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/dom-summary.json) describes HTML structure only; selecting an element is not a validated cleaner.
    35	
    36	## What remains to solve
    37	
    38	The two failures are established; a general repair is not. Simply accepting all extractor matches after headline filtering would expose some source text and some unrelated material. Restoring every long unmatched line would likewise mix body text with navigation, advertising and other stories. The earlier source-selection and adjacent-line experiments also had tradeoffs; see the [source provenance census](STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md), [source context observations](STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md), and [source-text comparison](STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md). Any proposal needs a complete source-preservation contract, source-role and ambiguity handling, tests against both recovered body text and contamination, and replay through the actual Step 1 path. Exact source location alone does not establish meaning. The frozen historical source and production settings remain unchanged.
    39	
    40	Claude proposed testing whether stripping trailing Unicode symbols, formatting and separators before the existing punctuation check would recover the article 16 paragraph. The [$0 full-corpus comparison](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/symbol-tail-two-sites-corpus-summary.json) applies that idea only at the two proposed headline-run and long-line-fallback checks, leaving the short-fragment check unchanged. The baseline reproduces all 6,463 saved fed-article texts. The candidate changes 80 pages, adding 124 lines and removing none, for 68,452 added characters. It recovers article 16 but does not recover article 2. On one EY page it adds 27 lines and 23,837 characters, including serialized HTML and page-navigation material. This contradicts the initial claim that only 24 lines would be affected and one promotional line added. The experiment is not adopted; its input change is not a count of useful facts or model errors. A preliminary broader probe also changed the short-fragment check and returned different numbers; the two-site result above is the relevant comparison of Claude's stated proposal. The full council is reviewing the result.
    41	
    42	Claude withdrew the symbol proposal in its [second response](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round2-response.md) and suggested examining the existing HTML paragraph/aside diagnostic. The [all-fed-article replay](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/html-v2-corpus-summary.json) reproduces all 49 previously saved diagnostic diffs and applies its same line-matching and page-tag logic across 6,463 originally fed articles. Thirty-two lack HTML; the candidate changes 1,054 pages, adding 2,816 line values and removing 296. It is a list of proposed changes, not constructed model inputs or a measure of accuracy. A sampled addition on `gnews:085fc8af40b0bd255bb43131` is a film story on a page whose source title concerns a BRICS summit, and other additions include promotional links. Some removals are bylines for related stories, but all removed lines require review in their source context before any claim of safety. The candidate also relies on the existing lossy ASCII matcher. The complete [per-page changes](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/html-v2-corpus.jsonl) are saved. The 49-article positive cases alone do not validate this approach.
    43	
    44	An independent reader checked a fixed sample of additions and removals against their pages. Codex confirmed the key examples: a report about AI video startup Higgsfield would gain several unrelated Bloomberg story headlines; a report on an NVIDIA investment would gain a free-report promotion; and a Cisco security article would gain a page tag and a separate article's introduction. Some sampled removals are bylines belonging to related articles. These examples establish both contamination and useful cleanup opportunities, not the overall precision of the candidate. The reader also noted that the control-count code would need revision if a future archive contained error rows; the current 49-row archive has no error rows.
    45	
    46	In a further source-backed counterexample, the candidate removes seven lines from `gnews:fb89ac7131700c42fdbec0ce`, an article about changes in AI training. They belong to an article-related timeline, including a reported Anthropic research-and-development measure and one accompanying sources line. Investment relevance remains a judgment, but the example shows that an `aside` can hold substantive article context; its HTML role alone does not establish that its contents are unrelated. Conversely, unrelated recommendation cards can be nested inside `main` and `article` elements, as in the BRICS page above. These counterexamples preclude adopting V2 based on tag ancestry alone.
    47	
    48	In its [third response](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round3-response.md), Claude agreed that HTML V2 cannot be used and proposed another structural selection using page tags and title matching. Its proposed exclusion of link-only lines, repeated publisher text and nested articles without a title match still treats structural hints as source boundaries. These are unvalidated judgments: links can be source disclosures, repeated text can be a current statement, and a nested article or aside can contain source context. The lossy ASCII lookup and multiple occurrences further weaken identity claims. Codex sent these objections and the full-page alternative for [another council challenge](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round4-handoff.txt). No structural selector has been adopted.
    49	
    50	In the [fourth response](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round4-response.md), Claude withdrew that structural selection after checking counterexamples, including article headers containing the author's name and publication date and linked text giving a source. The council favors testing full captured-page text as an isolated, one-call diagnostic without changing the current prompt in the first comparison; the existing clean text is the control. This is an untested hypothesis, not agreement that Luna can reliably identify the target article. The response proposes several known cases; its suggested BRICS source has only a `supply-chain-shift` theme tag, not the NVDA ticker, so it is not an eligible NVDA test item and must not be forced into that entity's context. Source selection and full evaluation details remain under review. The [diagnostic draft](STEP1-FULL-PAGE-DIAGNOSTIC.md) also records the independent review's cautions about fixed thesis states, incomplete captures, condition leakage and comparable citation checks.
    51	
    52	In the [fifth](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round5-response.md) and [sixth](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-council-2026-11-07/round6-response.md) rounds, Claude accepted the separate roles of known and previously selected samples and the exclusion of the non-NVDA BRICS item. It identified the months between the July article dates and the October page captures as an important limitation. The council agreed that later page material must be reported by source role and date where supported, without assuming that every later-dated passage is false or that an arm difference proves an internal model mechanism. The proposed 17-article comparison, including the longest page from the original 49, remains exploratory. Its execution design and source-first notes are being reviewed; no model comparison or production change has yet occurred.
    53	
    54	For a possible full-page comparison, the [source-only token census](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/page-budget-summary.json) measures the saved pages and cleaned text of all 6,463 originally fed articles using `o200k_base`. Median captured-page text is 2,015 tokens versus 770 cleaned; the 99th percentile is 7,573 versus 4,535; maxima are 28,516 versus 14,651. The 49-article replay has median 1,838 versus 690 and maximum 7,997 versus 5,506. These are source-field counts only, not complete model requests, model limits, costs or latency. A whole page can contain unrelated content, generated summaries, wrong captures or incomplete source; size alone cannot establish a safe input strategy.
    55	
    56	An offline [saved-answer check](../poc/data/clean-step1-source-loss-census-2026-11-07/full-page-saved-answer-replay.json) passed all 49 completed responses through the current isolated `runner.validate` using the saved clean text and then the full captured page. The same 248 of 250 passage instances located in both, the same two remained unlocated, and no located source string changed. This compares only location behavior for already returned quotations; it does not show what Luna would extract when sent the full page, whether a cited passage belongs to the target story, or whether a quote supports its summary. No forward run or state change occurred.
poc/data/clean-step1-context-restoration-2026-11-07/design.md:15:The 163 known affected records have bounded explicit source labels established by a separate census. Count whether each of their exact source-label positions is among the proposed positions and report affected records not covered. This is a diagnostic coverage number for those three known structures, not a fix guarantee and not a population estimate. Include the 200 other sampled articles to examine additional source text the approach would expose, its size and source diversity. Before computing or reviewing proposed content, choose 30 page IDs from the 363 by SHA-256 of UTF-8 `step1-context-review-2026-11-07`, newline and input_id, tie-breaking by ID. An independent reader inspects all proposed lines for those pages with full page context, including body, guidance and unrelated page material. Keep the selected IDs even when there are no proposals; this is a convenience review, not a population estimate. Do not use a category list to decide what the product keeps.
poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/round-26-response.md:13:| **C 行对齐元数据，不分类** | 纯行号不带标签文本，模型学不到标签；带被删行文本就是 A/B 的另一种排版 | 它的真正价值在读者和校验层：偏移、连续性旗子（已建，`lineage.jsonl` 有 `start/end`、候选行） | 无 | 若只给模型行号：零增长、零收益 | — | 作为模型输入不成立；作为复核层成立 |
poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/round-27-response.md:3:基本可以，但有两处缺口。每条记录已存：`page_lines`（去空行、带原始偏移，`probe.py:10-19`）、每个清洗行的 `source_candidates`（exact 可多个、joined_byline 两位置）、`mapping`、`extractor_text_match`（无抽取结果时为 None）、`byline_shape`、`long_sentence_shape`（`:112-114`），页级 `reproduced`、`extractor_state`、`stored_extractor_flag`、`html_sha256`。全部 15,280 行都有候选（15,026 + 237 + 17 = 15,280，零 unlocated）。所以“唯一精确锚点 + 未匹配位置 + 相邻 / 连续 run”完全可从 `lineage.jsonl` 算出，不必重跑抽取器。
poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/round-28-response.md:5:1. **one/run 两种模式的结果是构造决定的。** `replay_quotes.py:33` 把提案行按页序插进 baseline 行之间。一条在 baseline 里连续的引文，只有当插入点正好落在它跨越的两行之间才会变化。而 `probe.py:16` 只在 `extractor_text_match is False` 的长句旁找 anchor。Ising 页第 53 行与第 56 行都是 `extractor_text_match: true`（lineage.jsonl 第 126 条），54、55 两行永远不会被提议。"151 exact→exact"是必然，不是发现。
poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/round-28-response.md:56:- **T2 gap 清单**：从 lineage.jsonl 的 363 页列出每个 gap（起止页行、行数、字符）；对 163 个已知标签记录所在 gap 的行数；对 N ∈ {0,1,2,3,5,10,∞} 出两列：标签可见数（/163）、新增字符（/363 页）。
